WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.502
Relationships describe how many rows can connect between entities.

00:00:04.502 --> 00:00:10.732
In a one-to-one relationship, each side connects to at most one row on the other side.

00:00:10.732 --> 00:00:16.417
In a one-to-many relationship, one parent can connect to many child rows.

00:00:16.417 --> 00:00:22.217
A patient can have many appointments, so the foreign key belongs on the appointment side.

00:00:22.217 --> 00:00:27.597
In a many-to-many relationship, both sides can connect to many rows.

00:00:27.597 --> 00:00:33.402
Appointments can contain many services, and a service can appear in many appointments.

00:00:33.402 --> 00:00:38.807
Relational databases resolve that many-to-many relationship with a junction table.

00:00:38.807 --> 00:00:46.382
The placement rule is worth remembering conceptually: in one-to-many, put the foreign key on the many side.

00:00:46.382 --> 00:00:51.162
In many-to-many, introduce an intersection or junction table.

00:00:51.162 --> 00:00:55.872
Ask which side can exist many times for one row on the other side.

00:00:55.872 --> 00:00:59.722
That determines where the foreign key normally belongs.

00:00:59.722 --> 00:01:04.002
In an order system, many order lines belong to one order,

00:01:04.002 --> 00:01:07.472
so the order-line table carries the order identifier.

00:01:07.472 --> 00:01:12.400
The same reasoning applies to encounters, enrollments, and transactions.
